BT 1 0 0 1 0 315.804 Tm .5408 Tw 18 TL /F2 12 Tf .066667 .066667 .066667 rg (A: Nautica answers as a common carrier for the deaths, and it keeps neither the fortuitous-event) Tj T* 0 Tw (defense nor the limited-liability cap on these facts.) Tj  T* .546 Tw (Under Art. 1734 of the Civil Code, flood, storm, and like calamity excuse only when the carrier) Tj T* 0 Tw 1.495385 Tw (proves the excepted cause stood alone and that it observed extraordinary diligence. Under the) Tj T* 0 Tw 1.451 Tw (seaworthiness rule applied in Philippine American General Insurance Co. v. Court of Appeals) Tj T* 0 Tw 1.246909 Tw (\(G.R. No. 116940, 1997\), unfit hull conditions presume unseaworthiness, and ordinary voyage) Tj T* 0 Tw 1.8712 Tw (vicissitudes never excuse a ship that sailed unfit. Under Art. 587 of the Code of Commerce,) Tj T* 0 Tw 1.387714 Tw (abandonment of vessel and freight caps only the blameless owner whose captain alone was at) Tj T* 0 Tw (fault, never the owner whose own negligence concurred.) Tj  T* 2.647 Tw (Here, Nautica bought a second-hand passenger ship from Japan and modified the bulkhead) Tj T* 0 Tw 1.3824 Tw (design of the deck to accommodate more passengers, and the ship sank with its passengers in) Tj T* 0 Tw 4.430182 Tw (Tablas Strait during heavy monsoon rains. The bulkhead reconfiguration that may have) Tj T* 0 Tw 2.808 Tw (compromised stability puts sailing fitness in issue, and ordinary monsoon rains are voyage) Tj T* 0 Tw .308 Tw (vicissitudes rather than an irresistible calamity standing alone. Sailing a possibly unstable rebuilt) Tj T* 0 Tw .7152 Tw (ship with passengers aboard proves the opposite of the diligence the law demands, so the storm) Tj T* 0 Tw (defense fails at its first element.) Tj  T* .58875 Tw (Nautica therefore pays in full under the Civil Code without the Art. 587 cap, because the owner) Tj T* 0 Tw (fault that concurred in the casualty defeats both fortuity and limitation.) Tj T* ET
